# PART 5 — SMOOTH TRANSITIONS BETWEEN EVERYTHING

## 10. This is a major priority

I want the application to feel like one continuous building.

The user should not feel that they are navigating between unrelated web pages.

Examples:

### Grand Hall to Grand Stacks
The viewpoint moves toward the selected bookshelf, which grows in prominence until its books can be explored.

### Grand Stacks to an individual book
The selected book moves forward from the shelf, enlarges, and opens into its detail presentation.

### Grand Stacks back to Grand Hall
The view smoothly moves away from the bookshelf and restores the wider hall.

### Grand Hall to Acquisitions Room
The environment transitions through an appropriate doorway or corridor.

### Acquisitions Room to Wishlist book
The selected book is brought forward from the display and opens.

### Scholar's Study to Journal
The camera or view transitions toward the writing desk, and the journal opens.

### Archive Office to Backup
The selected ledger or archive cabinet opens into the backup interface.

These are examples of the intended visual language. Implement them in a way that feels cohesive and natural.

### Transition requirements

- Use smooth transitions rather than abrupt content replacement.
- Preserve the selected room and shelf context when returning.
- Avoid unnecessary loading screens.
- Avoid transitions that delay ordinary operations.
- Make transitions responsive and lightweight.
- Support reduced-motion settings.
- Ensure browser back navigation works sensibly.
- Ensure transitions work on both desktop and mobile.

Use a consistent transition system with reusable components or utilities.

Do not implement unrelated animations independently in every component.

The same visual language should be used throughout the application.

