# PART 6 — INTERACT WITH THE SURROUNDING ENVIRONMENT

## 11. Make the environment interactive

This is what I particularly want to improve.

I want users to be able to interact with more than just navigation buttons.

Add meaningful environmental interactions.

Examples:

| Environment object | Interaction |
|---|---|
| Bookshelf | Open a collection |
| Individual book | Open book details |
| Catalogue desk | Search the library |
| Acquisitions ledger | Open online book discovery |
| Reading journal | Open journal entries |
| Filing cabinet | Open data management |
| Archive ledger | Open backup and restore |
| Magical globe | Display collection statistics |
| Wall clock | Display the current local time |
| Library sign | Display room information |
| Floating book | Reveal a featured book or recently added item |
| Lantern | Toggle an appropriate visual lighting effect |
| Staircase | Navigate between library floors or areas |

You do not need to implement every example if it would be purely decorative or add unnecessary complexity.

Prioritize interactions that genuinely help users manage and explore their books.

### Environmental feedback

When users interact with objects:
- Highlight the object.
- Display a short descriptive label.
- Use subtle sound-free visual feedback by default.
- Animate the interaction.
- Provide a clear way to return.

Do not make the user guess what is clickable.

Use subtle hover states, small labels, contextual hints, and appropriate visual affordances.

For touch devices, use tapping and clear selection states.

### Important distinction

I want meaningful interactions, not random decorative gimmicks.

Every interactive object should either:
1. Perform a useful Book Tracker action.
2. Navigate to a relevant library area.
3. Display relevant information about the library or collection.

Do not add interactions that exist only to look impressive.

